---
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro';
import { company } from '@data/company';

const address = company.address;
---

<Base
  title="Privacy Policy | My Business Care Team"
  description="Privacy Policy for MyBCAT. Learn how we collect, use, and protect personal information when you visit mybcat.com, request services, or contact our team."
>
  <main id="main-content" class="legal-page">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="legal-header">
        <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
        <!-- REVIEW-01 (counsel): Set the effective date and the last updated date before publication. -->
        <p class="last-updated">Effective date: [to be set before publication]</p>
        <!-- REVIEW-02 (counsel): Remove this draft notice when the policy is approved for publication. -->
        <p class="draft-notice">Draft for legal review. This version is not in effect.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="legal-content">
        <section>
          <h2>1. About This Policy</h2>
          <!-- REVIEW-03 (owner): Confirm the legal entity name. It is taken from the site's Terms page. -->
          <p>This policy explains how My Business Care Team LLC ("MyBCAT," "we," "us," or "our") collects, uses, and shares personal information through mybcat.com and the forms, booking pages, and emails linked to it.</p>
          <p>Our website is for owners, managers, and staff of healthcare practices, mainly optometry practices, who are looking at our services for their business. It is not meant for patients.</p>
          <p>This policy does not cover information we handle for our client practices when we provide our services to them. Section 10 explains this.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>2. Information You Give Us</h2>
          <p>You give us information when you fill in a form, book a call, or email us. Each form marks the fields it needs to answer your request. The other fields are optional.</p>
          <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-labelledby="forms-caption" tabindex="0">
            <table>
              <caption id="forms-caption">What each part of our site asks for</caption>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Where</th>
                  <th scope="col">What we ask for</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Contact page and member pages</th>
                  <td>First and last name, work email, phone number, practice website, number of locations, and an optional message. Member pages also send a code that names the member program.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Conference event pages</th>
                  <td>First and last name, work email, and mobile phone number.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">ROI calculator</th>
                  <td>Name, work email, mobile phone number, company name, number of locations, the figures you enter, and the results the calculator shows.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Optometry practice growth study</th>
                  <td>Practice name and practice website. Phone number and email are optional.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Practice Growth onboarding</th>
                  <td>Practice name, city and state, website, owner name and email, the package you choose, number of offices, and notes. You can also send this brief from your own email program instead.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Confidential practice transition inquiry</th>
                  <td>First and last name, email, optional phone number, state, number of locations, transition timing, the role you want after a sale, and your consent choices.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Booking a call</th>
                  <td>We use Google Calendar appointment booking. Google's booking page asks for your name, your email, and any other questions on that page. Google passes the booking to us.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Email and phone calls</th>
                  <td>Whatever you choose to tell us.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p><strong>Please do not send patient information, health information, passwords, or financial account details</strong> through our website, forms, booking pages, or email.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>3. Information Collected Automatically</h2>
          <p>When you visit our site, your browser sends technical information to us and to the service providers in Section 5. This includes your IP address, browser and device type, operating system, the pages you view, the links and buttons you click, the time of your visit, the website that sent you, and any campaign tags in the link (for example, utm_source). An IP address can show your general location, such as your city or region.</p>
          <h3>Analytics</h3>
          <p>We use analytics tools to count visits and understand how people use the site. Some of these tools give your browser a random identifier and keep it in a cookie or in browser storage. Section 7 lists them.</p>
          <h3>Session recordings</h3>
          <!-- REVIEW-04 (owner): Confirm the PostHog session replay sample rate and whether Inspectlet records every visit. Confirm how Inspectlet is set to treat form fields (masked or not). The draft says only what the code shows: PostHog replay is on and masks form inputs; Inspectlet loads through Google Tag Manager. -->
          <p>Two of our tools, PostHog and Inspectlet, can record how a visit unfolds, such as page views, mouse movement, clicks, and scrolling. We set PostHog to hide what you type into form fields in these recordings.</p>
          <h3>Linking a visit to your email address</h3>
          <!-- REVIEW-05 (owner and counsel): Practice to change or disclose. The growth study page sends the visitor's email address to PostHog as the visitor's identifier after the form is sent. If this is removed before publication, delete this paragraph and the email reference in Section 5. -->
          <p>On the optometry practice growth study page, if you give us your email address, we also send it to PostHog. PostHog then links your earlier and later visits from that browser, including any session recordings, to your email address.</p>
          <h3>How you found us</h3>
          <p>Our own code stores a short record of how you first found our site: the campaign source, medium, and name; the website that sent you; the first page you saw; a channel label such as "search" or "social"; and the date. It stores the same fields for your current visit. We attach some of these values to the forms you send and to booking clicks, so we know which articles and campaigns bring inquiries. This record holds no name or email address.</p>
          <p>Some pages also keep a separate record with a random ID, a source category, and your first page. We use it to connect an inquiry to the article or page that led to it.</p>
          <h3>Advertising measurement</h3>
          <!-- REVIEW-06 (owner): Confirm whether the Google Ads account builds remarketing audiences from site visitors, and whether Google signals and ad personalization are on in Google Analytics. Both are set in Google's admin tools, not in the site code. The second sentence below assumes remarketing may be on. -->
          <p>We use Google Ads tags to measure whether our ads lead to visits and inquiries. Google may also use these tags to show our ads to people who have visited our site.</p>
          <h3>Tracking across websites</h3>
          <p>Google and the other analytics and advertising companies in Section 5 may collect information about your online activity over time and across different websites when you use our site.</p>
          <h3>Server logs</h3>
          <p>Our hosting provider and our form service keep standard request logs, such as IP address, time, page requested, and browser type. These logs help run and protect the site.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>4. How We Use Information</h2>
          <p>We use the information we collect to:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Answer your questions and arrange the calls you book</li>
            <li>Send you what you asked for, such as a growth plan, an event kit, or calculator results</li>
            <li>Follow up with you by email or phone about our services (you can opt out at any time)</li>
            <li>Review a confidential practice transition inquiry</li>
            <li>Understand how our site is used, fix problems, and improve it</li>
            <li>Measure which articles, campaigns, and ads bring inquiries</li>
            <li>Protect our site and prevent spam, fraud, and abuse</li>
            <li>Meet legal duties, enforce our agreements, and defend legal claims</li>
            <li>Plan or complete a merger, sale, or reorganization of our business</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>5. Who Receives Information</h2>
          <!-- REVIEW-07 (owner and counsel): Confirm that each provider below has a contract that limits its use of the data (service provider or processor terms). Google Ads and Google Analytics may use data for Google's own purposes depending on account settings. Confirm the provider that sends form confirmation emails and add it to the table. Confirm where the self-hosted CRM runs. Confirm where the transition form handler forwards submissions (its destination is set in a hosting setting, not in the code). -->
          <p>We use other companies to run our site and our business. The table shows each one and what it receives.</p>
          <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-labelledby="providers-caption" tabindex="0">
            <table class="wide">
              <caption id="providers-caption">Companies that receive information from our site</caption>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Company</th>
                  <th scope="col">What it does for us</th>
                  <th scope="col">What it receives</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Vercel</th>
                  <td>Hosts our website, runs Vercel Web Analytics, and runs one form handler</td>
                  <td>Request data (IP address, browser, pages), page views counted without cookies, and the contents of the confidential practice transition form</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Cloudflare</th>
                  <td>Runs our form intake service</td>
                  <td>The contents of our other website forms, the address of the page you sent it from, and campaign values</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Twenty (customer relationship software we run ourselves)</th>
                  <td>Stores inquiries and business contact records</td>
                  <td>Form contents and booking details</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Google Workspace and Google Calendar</th>
                  <td>Our booking pages, email, and calendars</td>
                  <td>The details you enter when you book, and emails you send us</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Google Tag Manager, Google Analytics, and Google Ads</th>
                  <td>Loads analytics and advertising tags, measures site use, and measures ad results</td>
                  <td>Pages viewed, clicks, device and browser data, IP address, cookie identifiers, and some site events</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">PostHog</th>
                  <td>Product analytics and session recordings</td>
                  <td>Page views, clicks, device and browser data, IP address, a random identifier, session recordings with form inputs hidden, campaign values, and on one page your email address (see Section 3)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Inspectlet</th>
                  <td>Session recordings</td>
                  <td>Pages viewed, mouse movement, clicks, scrolling, and device and browser data</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Ahrefs</th>
                  <td>Website traffic analytics</td>
                  <td>Page views, the website that sent you, and device and browser data</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">YouTube (Google)</th>
                  <td>Plays the video on our home page, only if you press play</td>
                  <td>Device and browser data and your viewing activity</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>We may also share information:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>With our professional advisers, such as lawyers, accountants, and auditors</li>
            <li>With government authorities or others when the law requires it, or to protect the rights and safety of you, us, or others</li>
            <li>With a buyer or successor if we are part of a merger, sale, or reorganization</li>
            <li>With anyone else when you ask us to or agree to it</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>6. Sale, Sharing, and Targeted Advertising</h2>
          <!-- REVIEW-08 (counsel): Decide how to describe the Google Analytics and Google Ads tags under state law ("sharing" for cross-context behavioral advertising, "targeted advertising," or "sale"). The draft discloses the possibility and offers an opt-out instead of saying "we do not sell or share." -->
          <p>We do not sell personal information for money. We do not rent or give your contact details to other companies for their own marketing.</p>
          <p>Our site uses Google Analytics and Google Ads tags. These tags let Google collect information about your visit, such as cookie identifiers, your IP address, and the pages you view. We use this information to measure our ads, and Google may use it to show our ads on other websites. Some state privacy laws call this "sharing" for cross-context behavioral advertising or "targeted advertising," and a few may treat it as a "sale." Section 8 explains how to opt out.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>7. Cookies and Browser Storage</h2>
          <p>Cookies and browser storage are small files and entries that a website keeps in your browser. Our site works without any of the items below. PostHog and our own records start when a page opens. Google Tag Manager starts after your first click, tap, or key press on a page, and it then loads the Google, Inspectlet, and Ahrefs tools.</p>
          <!-- REVIEW-09 (owner): Lifetimes are the values seen in a test visit to the live site on 2026-11-24. Vendors can change them. Re-check before publication. -->
          <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-labelledby="cookies-caption" tabindex="0">
            <table class="wide">
              <caption id="cookies-caption">Cookies and browser storage on mybcat.com</caption>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Name</th>
                  <th scope="col">Set by</th>
                  <th scope="col">Type</th>
                  <th scope="col">Purpose</th>
                  <th scope="col">How long it lasts</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row"><code>ph_[project key]_posthog</code></th>
                  <td>PostHog</td>
                  <td>Cookie on our domain</td>
                  <td>Random visitor ID and session ID for analytics</td>
                  <td>1 year</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Other items starting <code>ph_</code></th>
                  <td>PostHog</td>
                  <td>Local storage and session storage</td>
                  <td>The same visitor and session details, and recording state</td>
                  <td>Local storage: until you clear it. Session storage: until you close the tab.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row"><code>mybcat_first_touch_v1</code></th>
                  <td>MyBCAT</td>
                  <td>Local storage</td>
                  <td>How you first found us: campaign source, medium, and name; referring website; first page; channel; and date</td>
                  <td>No set end date. It stays until you clear your browser storage.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row"><code>mybcat_last_touch_v1</code></th>
                  <td>MyBCAT</td>
                  <td>Session storage</td>
                  <td>The same fields for your current visit</td>
                  <td>Until you close the tab</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row"><code>mybcat_attribution_v1</code></th>
                  <td>MyBCAT</td>
                  <td>Local storage</td>
                  <td>A random ID, a source category, and your first page, to connect an inquiry to the content that led to it</td>
                  <td>No set end date. It stays until you clear your browser storage.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row"><code>mybcat_oa_utm</code></th>
                  <td>MyBCAT</td>
                  <td>Session storage</td>
                  <td>Campaign tags on the growth study page</td>
                  <td>Until you close the tab</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row"><code>_ga</code> and <code>_ga_[ID]</code></th>
                  <td>Google Analytics</td>
                  <td>Cookie on our domain</td>
                  <td>Tell visitors and visits apart</td>
                  <td>About 13 months</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row"><code>_gcl_au</code> and <code>_gcl_ls</code></th>
                  <td>Google Ads</td>
                  <td>Cookie and local storage on our domain</td>
                  <td>Link ad clicks to visits and inquiries</td>
                  <td>Cookie: 90 days. Local storage: until you clear it.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row"><code>IDE</code> and <code>test_cookie</code></th>
                  <td>Google advertising (doubleclick.net)</td>
                  <td>Third-party cookie</td>
                  <td>Ad measurement and ad targeting</td>
                  <td><code>IDE</code>: about 13 months. <code>test_cookie</code>: a few minutes.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Items starting <code>__insp_</code></th>
                  <td>Inspectlet</td>
                  <td>Cookie on our domain</td>
                  <td>Session recording state and visit count</td>
                  <td>1 year</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>Vercel Web Analytics and Ahrefs did not set cookies in our test. Google may set its own cookies on its own websites when our booking calendar loads or when you play our video.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>8. Your Choices and Rights</h2>
          <h3>Global Privacy Control and Do Not Track</h3>
          <!-- REVIEW-10 (owner and counsel): Practice to change. Today only the lead source record obeys these signals. Several state laws require a business that is covered to treat Global Privacy Control as an opt-out of sale, sharing, or targeted advertising. If the site is changed to stop all analytics and advertising tags when a signal is present, replace this paragraph with a statement of the new behavior. -->
          <p>This is how our site responds to these browser signals today. If your browser sends a Global Privacy Control or Do Not Track signal, our site does not create the "how you found us" record (<code>mybcat_first_touch_v1</code> and <code>mybcat_last_touch_v1</code>) and removes any saved copy. At present, these signals do not switch off PostHog, Google Tag Manager, the Google, Inspectlet, and Ahrefs tools that Google Tag Manager loads, or the <code>mybcat_attribution_v1</code> record.</p>
          <h3>Opting out of analytics and advertising</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Block or delete cookies and site data in your browser settings. Our site still works.</li>
            <li>Use Google's opt-out add-on for Google Analytics at <a href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout" target="_blank" rel="noopener">tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout</a>.</li>
            <li>Manage Google ad personalization at <a href="https://myadcenter.google.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">myadcenter.google.com</a>.</li>
            <li>Email us to opt out of any sale, sharing, or targeted advertising. We will apply your request to the information we hold about you.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3>Marketing emails and calls</h3>
          <p>Each marketing email we send has an unsubscribe link. You can also email us to stop marketing emails or calls. We may still contact you about a request you made.</p>
          <h3>Text messages</h3>
          <!-- REVIEW-11 (owner): Confirm whether MyBCAT sends text messages to website visitors at all. If it never does, replace this paragraph with one sentence: "We do not send text messages to phone numbers collected on this site." If it does, confirm the program name, the consent wording on each form, and the help contact. -->
          <p>We use the phone number you give us to contact you about your request. We do not send marketing text messages unless you separately agree to receive them. If you agree, message frequency varies, message and data rates may apply, you can reply STOP to stop, and you can reply HELP for help. We do not share mobile phone numbers or text message consent with other companies for their marketing.</p>
          <h3>Access, correction, deletion, and other rights</h3>
          <!-- REVIEW-12 (counsel): Decide whether to offer these rights to everyone (as drafted) or only where a state law applies. Confirm the response time and the appeal process. -->
          <p>Depending on where you live, you may have the right to:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Know what personal information we have about you, and how we use and share it</li>
            <li>Get a copy of your information in a format you can move</li>
            <li>Correct information that is wrong</li>
            <li>Delete your information</li>
            <li>Opt out of the sale or sharing of your information, targeted advertising, and certain profiling</li>
          </ul>
          <p>You can make a request even if your state does not give you these rights, and we will consider it.</p>
          <p>To make a request, email privacy (at) mybcat (dot) com. Tell us what you want and the email address you used with us. We will check your identity, usually by contacting you at that email address. An authorized agent can make a request for you with your written permission. We will not treat you differently for using your rights.</p>
          <p>We will respond within the time the law requires. If we turn down your request, you can ask us to look at it again by replying to our decision. If you disagree with the result, you can contact the attorney general of your state.</p>
          <h3>Details for state privacy laws</h3>
          <p>In the past 12 months, we collected the categories of personal information below. Sections 2 and 3 describe the sources, and Section 4 describes the purposes. We do not ask for sensitive personal information on our forms.</p>
          <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-labelledby="categories-caption" tabindex="0">
            <table class="wide">
              <caption id="categories-caption">Categories of personal information</caption>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Category</th>
                  <th scope="col">Examples</th>
                  <th scope="col">Who receives it</th>
                  <th scope="col">Sold or shared?</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Identifiers</th>
                  <td>Name, email, phone number, IP address, cookie and browser identifiers</td>
                  <td>The companies in Section 5</td>
                  <td>Not sold for money. IP address and cookie identifiers may be "shared" with Google for advertising (Section 6).</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Professional information</th>
                  <td>Practice name, website, number of locations, role, and transition plans</td>
                  <td>Vercel, Cloudflare, our customer relationship software, and Google Workspace</td>
                  <td>No</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Internet activity</th>
                  <td>Pages viewed, clicks, referring website, campaign tags, and session recordings</td>
                  <td>Our analytics and advertising providers in Section 5</td>
                  <td>May be "shared" with Google for advertising (Section 6)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">General location</th>
                  <td>City or region estimated from your IP address</td>
                  <td>Our hosting, analytics, and advertising providers</td>
                  <td>May be "shared" with Google for advertising (Section 6)</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>9. How Long We Keep Information</h2>
          <!-- REVIEW-13 (owner and counsel): Set a retention period, or the criteria for one, for each category: inquiries and contact records, booking details, transition inquiries, analytics data (Google Analytics retention setting, PostHog plan retention, Inspectlet plan retention), and server logs (Vercel and Cloudflare plan settings). The draft states only what the code or vendor documents show. -->
          <ul>
            <li>Browser storage and cookies last as shown in Section 7.</li>
            <li>Vercel Web Analytics discards the visitor session it uses for counting after 24 hours.</li>
            <li>We keep inquiries, booking details, and contact records while we are talking with you or providing services, and then for as long as we need them for business records and legal duties. After that, we delete them or remove the details that identify you.</li>
            <li>Our analytics and session recording providers keep data for the period set in our account with each provider.</li>
            <li>Our hosting providers keep server logs for a short period set by their service.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>10. Information We Handle for Our Client Practices</h2>
          <!-- REVIEW-14 (counsel): The old policy said MyBCAT operates as a HIPAA Business Associate and complies with all applicable HIPAA regulations. The draft makes no HIPAA claim. Counsel may choose to add one sentence such as "Where HIPAA applies, we sign a business associate agreement with the practice," if that is true for every client. -->
          <p>MyBCAT provides front desk, answering, scheduling, and related back-office services to healthcare practices. When we do this work, we may handle information about a practice's patients. We handle that information for the practice and under our written agreement with that practice. This policy does not apply to it.</p>
          <p>If you are a patient of one of our client practices, please contact that practice with questions about your information. Their privacy notice explains how they use it.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>11. Security</h2>
          <!-- REVIEW-15 (owner): The draft names only HTTPS because the code shows it. Add other safeguards (for example access limits on the customer relationship software) only if they are true today. -->
          <p>We use reasonable safeguards to protect the information we collect through this site. For example, our site and our forms use encrypted connections (HTTPS). No website, transmission, or storage system is completely secure, so we cannot promise that your information will always be safe.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>12. Children</h2>
          <!-- REVIEW-16 (counsel): Choose the age. The draft uses 16 to match state laws that give extra protection to teenagers. Under 13 is the minimum for COPPA. -->
          <p>Our website is for businesses. It is not directed to children, and we do not knowingly collect personal information from anyone under 16. We do not sell or share the personal information of anyone we know is under 16. If you think a child has sent us information, email us and we will delete it.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>13. Visitors Outside the United States</h2>
          <!-- REVIEW-17 (counsel): Choose one of two options. Option B (below, shown) says the site is directed to the United States. Option A adds a short GDPR and UK GDPR section. The site's separate GDPR page makes claims (Standard Contractual Clauses, a data protection officer, 26 month analytics retention) that the code does not show. Align or retire that page with whichever option is chosen.

          Option A text, for use instead of Option B:
          "If you are in the European Economic Area or the United Kingdom. My Business Care Team LLC is the controller of the personal information described in this policy. We rely on these legal bases: your consent (for analytics and advertising cookies, and for marketing where consent is required); steps you ask us to take before a contract; our legitimate interests in answering business inquiries, promoting our services, and protecting our site; and legal obligations. You have the rights to access, correct, delete, restrict, and move your information, to object to processing based on legitimate interests and to direct marketing, and to withdraw consent at any time. We transfer information to the United States and use [transfer mechanism to be confirmed] to protect it. You can complain to your local data protection authority, or in the United Kingdom to the Information Commissioner's Office. [EU or UK representative, if required.]"
          Option A also needs a consent tool that holds analytics and advertising tags until a visitor in those regions agrees. -->
          <p>We are based in the United States, and our services are for healthcare practices in the United States. Our website is meant for visitors in the United States. If you visit from another country, your information will be handled in the United States, where privacy laws may differ from the laws where you live. You can still send us a privacy request from anywhere.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>14. Links to Other Websites</h2>
          <p>Our site links to other websites, such as podcast apps, social networks, and booking pages hosted by Google. Their own privacy policies apply when you visit them. We are not responsible for how they handle information.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>15. Changes to This Policy</h2>
          <!-- REVIEW-18 (counsel): Confirm the advance notice commitment and the yearly review. -->
          <p>We review this policy at least once a year. When we change it, we post the new version on this page and change the effective date at the top. If we make a material change, we will tell you in a clearer way before the change takes effect, for example with a notice on our site or by email. We will not apply a material change to information we already have without your consent where the law requires consent.</p>
        </section>

